I've been playing NetHack extensively recently, and I've been posting
quite a bit about the demises of my various characters. But now for
something a bit more light-hearted:

   My most recent wizard was a human follower of Thoth, who didn't do
anything special, really, but managed to bring into the dungeon a magic
marker and a scroll of genocide. After an uneventful bout, he ran into
some trouble with an excess of water moccasins, and remembering the
demise of a previous wizard (who had depleted his mana, wand of fire,
and his health, and fell to the one remaining moccasin), immediately
purged them from the game. Another scroll of genocide was written for
an emergency, and this came up in minetown, where this wizard ran into
a bit of trouble against a couple of uruks in minetown, and with about
10 health left and no more mana, exterminated the orc-folk of Saruman
in the dungeon as well.
    While exploring the mines, this wizard stumbled upon not one, but
two additional red '(' lying upon the ground. He gladly picked them up,
but kept in mind that only once before had the player actually used up
a full magic marker before sending his character to its end.
Surprisingly, while going deeper into the dungeon, the wizard found a
bones file that his bretheren had made earlier that day on the same
public server, and gladly took the scrolls (and another magic marker)
from that wizard's rotting body. Returning to the main branch of the
dungeon, he found that a few soldier ants were following him, and
seeing as his physique was measly, soon sent all soldier ants to their
doom as well.
    Armed with the knowledge of magic missile that he found at mine's
end, this wizard proceeded through the main dungeon, finding an altar
every two or three levels, and gladly sacrificed there, though recieved
nothing from an apathetic Thoth. Eventually, after boring of the
endless sacrifice, the wizard reverse-genocided nurses to bulk up his
measly 60-or-so HP to a tough 130. Hearing from somewhere that succubi
granted good bonuses, as well as the rumour that they were good to
reverse-genocide, he blanked an unidentified scroll and wrote on it
with a cursed magic marker. He was quite surprised when he was told
that it would not be done, and deciding that he must have blanked a
blessed scroll, used this uncursed genocide on the class of E, or
rather, those pesky invisible stalkers.
    "Sent in some stalkers."
    Alas, it was the end of this wizard.... or at least his continued
visibility! Magic missiles fired in all directions and bouncing off the
walls of the room produced enough carnage to slaughter everything in
the room, visible or not, with the exception of our magic-protected
wizard. Even when a black unicorn caught him unawares while feasting on
stalker meat, a reflected missile was able to finish off that creature
and grant him a unicorn horn, which made the game immensely easy from
then on. With permanent invisibility and the ability to see the
invisible, the wizard marched on, blasting away with his missiles.
    Clearing out the quest level and its wraiths brought the wizard to
level 15, so that every level gained after 12 was from wraith corpses
or potions of gain level. Neferet the Green gave him the quest, and he
left the home level to seek the Dark Tower. In the Rogue level, the
wizard found an elf zombie with a name and a warning level of 3.
Backing up to the staircase, he was able to defeat this menace with
unexpected ease, and took from the remains several good objects,
including an amulet of reflection. The level above had another altar,
so he identified the cursed items and stashed them away in a chest.
    Three or four levels later, he encountered yet another unfamiliar
bones file, in quite a crowd of nasties. Fortunately, this wizard had
already genocided V, c, L, soldier ants, uruk-hai, and water moccasins,
so the crowd was probably thinner for him, and from the pile he
recovered a robe, another amulet of reflection, as well as several
quite useful spellbooks, granting him the charm monster spell.
    Whereas the wizard found that mana was often a problem; whereas he
still needed magic resistance; whereas heard that the quest artifact
gave faster mana regeneration and magic resistance; and whereas he
still hadn't found the tower that Neferet was talking about, therefore
he returned to the quest level and found a secret staircase, proceeding
down to the dark, dark, dungeons with the Frost Brand that he had found
lying about and not the -1 Sting that he had dropped a while ago, and
then proceeding to fall into a puddle of water outside of the tower and
drowning.
    If it weren't for the amulet of magical breathing that he was
wearing, anyway. So with a rusty Frost Brand and all of his scrolls
blanked except 3 of confuse monster, he went on to face the Dark One,
who would bring him to his doom... if he hadn't also found in the last
bones file (which he had cancelled after dragging out all the armour) a
+2 silver dragon scale mail (which he'd merged with a while ago,
destroying the robe from the bones file and the +0 t-shirt that he'd
found -- but luckily turning back a few hundred turns later, and not
biting a cockatrice this time, as they were genocided), giving him
plenty of protection. The Dark One kept bouncing around and running up
staircases, but he eventually chased him down and took from him the Eye
of the Aethopica. Finishing the quest, the wizard proceeded to write
more scrolls, and after making Frost Brand glow a mottled purple the
first time, was able to rustproof it.
     Eventually he tamed a sleeping Jabberwock on the fourth try, and
took it down with him to clear a zoo. Unfortunately, it kept shying
away, despite attempts to displace it into corridors while leashed, and
an orange dragon showed up with a sleep blast. Oh well, better luck
next time. For the dragon, for this wizard had snacked on elves and
anyways, had reflection, so he zapped the dragon a few times and down
it went. The rest of the zoo was easy, and he proceeded down to the
next level, which was a swamp. It's a shame that he didn't start out
with a ring of free action... but actually, the staircase was just in
the next room, and both being dry, the wizard didn't encounter any sea
creatures at all except through telepathy, and instead later genocided
all ;.
      Soon there was a level filled with water, and telepathy revealed
that the dreaded Medusa was hiding on the other side of the sea,
surrounded with numerous nasties. But first, the wizard had to deal
with that annoying titan that kept on summoning dragons right next to
him. However, the chamber was so filled with creatures that he was
overwhelmed, and he never got the chance to kill the 'H', for while he
was fighting the pair of dragons beside him (and genociding that
disenchanter that was also there), a green 'P' identified as a green
slime came up and... well, you can guess.
     While the wizard was busy taking care of the top of the room, his
pet jabberwock was fighting just out of sight below, and killed the
titan as well as the green slime just in time. Finally, the room was
cleared, and the wizard pushed the titan's pet rock over the middle of
the three pools in the room where he guessed the hidden door would be.
And in fact, there was a door there, and he walked out and over...
actually, no. Outside the door was more water, and he didn't have a
means of going over it. So down was the only option, and he pulled out
his pickaxe and dug a hole, pulling his jabberwock with him.
Conveniently, he found that the staircase was nearby -- in fact, both
of them were, quite close to eachother. So, putting on his blindfold,
he dragged his jabberwock with him upstairs to deal with Medusa.
    "You have a strange sad feeling for a moment, then it passes."
     Oh. Oops. Well, the jabberwock was slow anyway...
     Medusa saw her own reflection in a nymph's mirror and turned to
stone, and the wizard chopped apart the statue of Perseus, as well as
the statue of some other random guy, but only Perseus' contained items.
Seems that players and monsters don't leave their items after being
stoned, only Perseus.... Well, he had a sack, some boots of levitation,
and a scimitar, so with the sack and the boots, the wizard went on his
way, finding time to write a spellbook of identify to help him along,
having by this time uncursed his two magic markers, blessed one,
charged some, and gaining disintegration and shock resistance.
     Along came the castle, where there was a whorde of gremlins and a
silver dragon, and the wizard shot the former and tamed the latter and
left it there. At the drawbridge he rang the Bell of Opening a couple
of times, to no avail, so he blasted the drawbridge with his
almost-forgotten force bolt, and just lobbed magic missiles inside
until things stopped coming out. A soldier had nicely shot a bolt of
cold at him, which not only killed several things inside when it
reflected, but also provided a nice bridge across the moat.

